  "text_md": "A subrepresentation of a representation V of the magnetic point group (G,6) is a subspace W c such that the restriction of the G-action to W is also a representation of (G, 0) A representation Vof (G, 0) is irreducible if its only representations are V and 0.\n\nNow; the category of representations of finite magnetic groups is semisimple.\n\nProposition 1.9. Every representation of a finite magnetic group is a sum of irreducible representations.\n\nWhich follows from Maschke's lemma to representations of magnetic groups. applied\n\nLemma 1.10 (Maschke) If W is a subrepresentation of V of a finite magnetic group; then there exists a subrepresentation W\n\nProof: Write V as a direct sum V = WeU (with U not necessary a subrepresentation) and consider the projection T1 : V \\_ W given by T1(w + u) = w. Define\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe morphism T is in\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nby W' = ker T, then we have the decomposition as a direct sum of representations of (G,0)\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nOne step required to classify the irreducible representations of magnetic groups (G,0) is to construct the induced representations of the core Go &lt; G. key\n\nDefinition 1.11. Let (G,0) be a finite magnetic point group and V a representation of the core group Go. The induced representation of V is the complex vector space\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->",
